     "color": "#000000"
    },
    {
     "kind": "text",
     "text": "Prompt: \u201cA lion isolated on a solid green background\u201d",
     "rect": [
      30,
      325.7265415549598,
      900,
      32
     ],
     "size": 22,
     "color": "#000000"
    },
    {
     "kind": "text",
     "text": "The model decides everything you didn't say.",
     "rect": [
      30,
      430,
      900,
      50
     ],
     "size": 30,
     "color": "#C00000"
    }
   ],
   "speech": "Say I want a cutout of a lion. So I ask for a lion isolated on a solid green background, then I key out the green. Five big models, and none of these can be keyed cleanly. Some backgrounds aren't solid, some lions turn green, one puts him in a field. That's the core problem. A prompt is a handful of words, the output is millions of pixels, so the model decides everything you didn't say, and it decides with what IT likes, not what you wanted. We'll come back to this lion in MAGICK."
  },
  {
   "title": "One question",
   "elements": [
    {
     "kind": "text",
     "text": "How do you control a pretrained diffusion model without building a new one?",
     "rect": [
      40,
      200,
      880,
      120
     ],
     "size": 40,
     "color": "#C00000"
    }
   ],
   "speech": "So here's the one question my thesis answers: how do you get real control over a big pretrained diffusion model without building a new one? Each paper adds one handle the model never had. You steer the pretrained model, you don't rebuild it."
  },
